if you want to learn german check out  german uncovered a course which uses the  power of story learning to take you from  a beginner level all the way to  intermediate  click the link in the description for  more information  learners of german are often surprised  to discover just how different spoken  german can be depending on where you go  what we call german includes both  standard german and a diverse range of  dialects that differ throughout the  german-speaking regions  one of the most famous examples of this  is swiss german the dialect  spoken in switzerland they're often  different enough that speakers of  standard german can't understand them  swiss german is really a geographic  grouping of all the dialects spoken in  switzerland  rather than a linguistic grouping there  are cases in which swiss german dialects  are more similar to dialects spoken  across the border in germany  austria liechtenstein or south t-roll  than they are to other swiss dialects  that are further away  the dialects of switzerland are alamanic  dialects which is a subgroup of upper  german  there are four subgroups within alemanic  swabian  low alemannic high alamanic and highest  alamanic  it's a dialect continuum getting further  from standard german as you move  southward  in switzerland there are mainly high  alemannic and highest alamanic dialects  with a pocket of low alemanic around the  city of basel  there's quite a lot of variation amongst  swiss german dialects the speaker in  this video comes from the zurich area  in switzerland people almost exclusively  use their dialect in casual situations  as opposed to in germany where there's  been more of a shift towards standard  german  in formal situations and in writing they  use swiss standard german which is  almost the same as the standard german  used in germany with some minor  differences  there is no sharp s to represent the  sound si  it's written with double s instead some  different vocabulary is used yes even in  the written language  for example reservation instead of  reservieron  meaning hospital instead of standard  german krankenhaus  velo meaning bicycle instead of standard  german  farad meaning skirt  instead of standard german rook these  words are borrowings from french  but the real differences lie in the  swiss german dialects  including a lot of different words hello  grizzy  this is a contraction of the old polite  expression  meaning i greet you bye audi  instead of standard german choose excuse  me  excuse me this comes from french excuse  me thank you merci instead of standard  german  danke or one of its variants this word  comes from french of course  another option is merciful  means many times a little bit spitzerly  rather than standard german ambition  in standard german the verb meaning to  run is laufen  in swiss german there's the verb laufe  but it actually means  walk the verb meaning to run in swiss  german is  rane it has a cognate and standard  german  which is used more with the sense of to  race or to move quickly  by the way notice that the standard  german infinitive form of verbs ends in  n while the swiss german infinitives  don't have the n at the end  the verb meaning to work in standard  german albeit  in swiss german in standard german this  verb normally means to create  or to manage to do something those are  just a few examples there are far too  many different  words to list them all here  pronunciation one difference is that  alemanic dialects  for the most part fully underwent the  high german consonant shift  a change in consonant sounds that  occurred during the development of old  high german standard german has some of  these changes but not all  one change that most swiss dialects  underwent but standard german didn't  was the shift from ka to kha or  at the beginning of words when doubled  after r  or l and after m or n for example  thanks in standard german danke and in  swiss german  child in standard german kind and in  swiss german  that the vowel is also different being  raised in swiss german  there are sometimes differences between  monophthongs and diphthongs  listen to the words for house and pay  attention to the vowel sounds  in standard german house and in swiss  german  whose swiss german dialects did not  undergo the new high german  diphthongization  in which certain vowels became  diphthongs swiss german maintains the  monophthongs of middle high german  the long vowels  to say my house in standard german you  would say meinhouse  while in swiss german you would say miss  the plural houses in standard german  would be horizon  in swiss german it would be  conversely there are some diphthongs  that became monophthongs in standard  german  but remain diphthongs in swiss german  and each with a schwa in second position  for example the word for love in  standard german it's  liber while in swiss german it's lipi  the word for good in standard german  it's good  while in swiss german it's with the word  for foot  in standard german it's fuse and in  swiss german it's  fuss grammar another difference is that  swiss german does not have a preterit  tense  instead they use the perfect tense for  example the sentence meaning  yesterday i was at home in standard  german it's  guest unvised zuhausen word for word  it's yesterday was  i at home in swiss german it's gesture  high xc word for word that's yesterday  am i  at home bean word for word in standard  german this would be  gaston binis tsuhausegeven but it would  literally mean  yesterday i have been at home note that  e  is an unstressed form of ich when it  comes after the verb  you'll probably also notice differences  in the forms of verbs  an example that might have jumped out at  you is standard german  given versus swiss german c  they are both the past participle of the  verb meaning to be  in swiss german the verb meaning to go  is doubled when used before another verb  for instance if you want to say i go  traveling you would say  the first go here is the first person  singular present tense form  and the second one is the infinitive in  standard german you would say  a construction like this is also used  for the verbs meaning to come  and begin  is the singular imperative form and ho  is the infinitive another difference  lies in the diminutive suffix which  expresses a smaller  cuter or younger version of the noun in  standard german it's  line or hen while in swiss german it's  li in standard german a small mouse is  moist line in swiss german it's muesli  this could result in some funny  confusion because in standard german  muesli refers to muesli the kind of food  in swiss german the food is called  muesli  this difference is a result of one of  the sound shifts i mentioned earlier  the diphthong became a monophthong in  standard german  a little house in standard german it's  hoistline  or hoisin while in swiss german it's  oozly next rather than all the different  relative pronouns used in standard  german  in swiss german there's only one  relative clause marker  vo for example with the meaning of the  friend that i always  go drinking with in standard german  their friend with  emma's outfinger word for word it's the  friend  with whom i always drink infinitive  go in swiss german it's different  word for word it's the friend that i  always  with him go go drink this form is a  contraction of  vo and ich or its unstressed form  e notice that the swiss german relative  clause  has something called a resumptive  pronoun a pronoun that restates the  person or thing that you're talking  about  him refers back to different the  standard german relative clause doesn't  have a resumptive pronoun  now let's look at some sentences and see  what we find these sentences mean  we went fishing and caught lots of fish  in standard german  vagina and fingerfield fisher word for  word it's  we went fishing and caught lots fish  in swiss german it's missing a  handfulfish crown  word for word it's we are go infinitive  fish infinitive gone and have  lots fish caught first notice the  different first-person plural pronouns  the swiss german pronoun resembles the  first singular dative pronoun  in both standard german and swiss german  next notice that the standard german  sentence has the preterit tense  or the simple past tense while the swiss  german sentence uses the perfect tense  instead  as we saw before and again we see that  the verb meaning go  is doubled when used with another verb  in the verb phrase meaning go fishing  the word for fishing is also different  in standard german the usual word is  angel while in swiss german it's fiche  these sentences mean i want to buy the  kitten we saw at the pet shop  in standard germany's catsyan coffin  that's the entertainer  word for word it's i want the kitten by  infinitive that we in the pet shop  scene have in swiss german it is a hand  word for word it's i want the kitten by  infinitive that we in the pet shop  scene have first notice the different  words meaning  want vil in standard german and veit  in swiss german next we see that the  definite article is shortened in swiss  german  then notice the different diminutive  forms of the word for cat  which mean kitten in standard german  ketzgen  and in swiss german hatzli and again  notice that the initial consonant is  different  k in standard german and in swiss german  we see that same difference in kaufen  and alpha  and again we see that the swiss german  infinitive lacks the final  n next again we see the different  relative clause marker in swiss german  vo in that it again contracts with the  personal pronoun  vo plus mir becomes vomer  after that we see ide meaning in the  written as one word  then we see a slightly different form of  the word for pet shop  then we see different forms of the past  participle of the verb meaning to see  and again we see that the present tense  of the auxiliary verb meaning to have  has a different form of the first person  plural conjugation  as you can see swiss german dialects are  fairly different from standard german  of course they're closely related and if  swiss german had a different name  like just swiss we'd probably say wow  swiss and german are so similar  but because it's called swiss german the  differences stand out to us  remember that swiss german dialects are  alamanic dialects which are part of the  upper german branch of high german  some people believe that alemanic and  also bavarian  are different languages from standard  german but because they're closely  related to it and because standard  german  is the written and formal language  that's used in combination with them  they are normally considered part of the  german language  the question of the day for german  speakers outside of switzerland  how well do you understand swiss german  how different is it from your usual way  of speaking  whether that's standard german or  dialect and for swiss german speakers  how easy is it to communicate  with german speakers from other parts of  switzerland and those from outside of  switzerland  do you usually use standard german with  speakers of different dialects  if you're excited to learn german and to  be clear i mean standard german  i highly recommend you check out the  course german uncovered that i mentioned  at the beginning of the video  german uncovered was created by ollie  richards a major figure in the language  learning community who pioneered a  unique approach to language learning  using stories  you may have seen some of his  best-selling books at your local  bookshop or on amazon  his uncovered courses are based on the  same philosophy of story learning but  they're much more extensive and are  designed to take you from a beginner  level  up to the b1 level which is lower  intermediate  the course consists of 20 chapters of a  story with each chapter containing not  only a core reading and listening  section but also additional video  lessons and exercises that build on the  core material  if you're tired of boring apps and  simple grammar-based translation  try the story learning approach with  german uncover click the link in the  description for more information  right now it's time to say thanks to the  langfocus patreon supporters  especially these ones right here on the  screen because they are the top tier  patreon supporters  langfocus patrons make it much easier to  keep this channel going month after  month so many special thanks to them  and to everyone thank you for watching  and have a nice day  you

